{"kind": "report", "step": 17, "ts": "2026-11-19T01:24:15.493053+00:00", "data": {"category": "dns", "host": "gw.lan", "component": "service_config", "cause": "The firewall's unbound applies DNS-rebinding protection (private-address: 10.0.0.0/8) to answers it forwards for .lan, so legitimate local records such as nas.lan = 10.20.0.50 come back as an empty NOERROR answer (i.e. \"not found\") whenever a client ends up querying gw.lan; that happens intermittently because dns1.lan's rate limit (20 q/s for the whole 10.20.0.0/24) drops query bursts and pushes clients over to the second server.", "evidence": ["mon.lan /etc/resolv.conf lists nameserver 10.20.0.3 then 10.20.0.1 with 'search lan', so both resolvers are used by getaddrinfo.", "dig @10.20.0.3 nas.lan A -> NOERROR with ANSWER: nas.lan. 10 IN A 10.20.0.50 (query time 1 msec) - dns1 does know the name.", "dig @10.20.0.1 nas.lan A -> NOERROR but ANSWER: 0, AUTHORITY: 0, ADDITIONAL: 1, MSG SIZE 36 - gw.lan returns a no-data answer rather than NXDOMAIN or the address.", "drill nas.lan @127.0.0.1 on gw.lan itself also returns rcode NOERROR with ANSWER: 0, so the empty answer is produced locally by the resolver, not by an unreachable upstream.", "cat /var/unbound/unbound.conf on gw.lan shows 'private-address: 10.0.0.0/8' commented as 'DNS rebinding protection: drop answers for public names that point at private addresses', plus 'forward-zone: name \"lan.\" forward-addr: 10.20.0.3' and only 'local-zone: \"local.\" static' - nothing exempts the .lan data from the private-address filter.", "dns1.lan config.yaml (/opt/primary-dns/config.yaml) rewrites *.lan and nas.lan to 10.20.0.50 and sets ratelimit: 20 with ratelimit_subnet_len_ipv4: 24 - one shared bucket for all clients.", "journalctl -u primary-dns on dns1.lan shows repeated short bursts: 'ratelimit: 10.20.0.0/24 exceeded 20 q/s, dropping 23 queries' at 02:14:00 with 'back under limit' at 02:14:01, and again at 09:41 and 13:02 - brief drops matching alerts that self-clear within a minute or two."], "action": "change_config", "recommendation": "On gw.lan, stop the rebinding filter from swallowing local data: exempt the .lan zone from private-address filtering (e.g. serve the local names as unbound local-data/local-zone for \"lan.\" on the firewall, or add an allow exception so forwarded 10.20.0.0/8 answers for .lan are returned). Then verify with drill/host against 10.20.0.1 that nas.lan returns 10.20.0.50 instead of an empty answer. Secondarily, raise ratelimit / use a finer ratelimit_subnet_len_ipv4 on dns1.lan so normal bursts stop being dropped; public lookups need no change since they resolve to non-private addresses.", "detail": "unbound private-address filtering hides the .lan answers", "contributing": {"host": "dns1.lan", "component": "dns_rate_limit"}}}
